.TH CBMAP_GET_ELEM_REF 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cbmap_get_elem_ref \- get reference to value by key
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

ccol_retval_t cbmap_get_elem_ref(cbmap cbm,
                                  const cmap_pair *key_pair,
                                  const cmap_pair **val_pair);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Gets a reference to the value of the given key. The reference points into
the internal storage. The caller can read the value through it. The
caller can also change the bytes of the value in place.

The out-parameter is a const cmap_pair **. This is why the caller must
declare its own variable as const cmap_pair *. The address of a plain
cmap_pair * does not compile.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cbm        BST map to search
key_pair   Key to look up
val_pair   Address of a const cmap_pair * that receives the reference
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        - The function found the key, and *val_pair points
                      to the value
ccol_key_not_found  - The key is not in the map
ccol_invalid_args   - key_pair or val_pair is NULL, key_pair carries a
                      NULL ptr with a non-zero size, or the size of
                      key_pair does not match the size of a fixed-width
                      key type (see cbmap_create_full(3))
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The ptr of a key_pair needs no particular alignment. The comparison
reads the key bytes with memcpy and never through a pointer cast to the
key type. A caller can therefore point key_pair->ptr straight at key
bytes that sit at an arbitrary offset inside a larger buffer, such as a
field sliced out of a packed network frame or a memory-mapped record,
without copying them into an aligned local first.
The returned cmap_pair is the accessor of the map for that element. It
describes the value, and it is not part of the value. Its target is
const-qualified. The caller can read through val_pair->ptr. The caller
can also write to the bytes that it points at, within val_pair->size.
But an assignment to val_pair->ptr or to val_pair->size is a compile
error. The pointer and the size describe one another. The map cannot
own a pointer that it did not allocate. To replace a value, use
cbmap_insert_elem(3).
O(log n) complexity
The reference stays valid until the next insert or delete
The function asserts if cbm is NULL
The function reports a NULL key_pair or a NULL val_pair as
ccol_invalid_args. This matches chmap_get_elem_ref(3)
A key_pair whose ptr is NULL and whose size is not zero promises bytes
that it has no storage for. The function reports it as
ccol_invalid_args too
A key_pair of size zero is correct, with a ptr or without one. It looks
up the empty key
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_get (3),
.BR cbmap_get_ptr (3),
.BR cbmap_insert_elem (3)
